{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/962750-1e-newbie-questions","id":962750,"title":"1E Newbie Questions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-03T03:20:50-07:00","id":962750,"title":"1E Newbie Questions","content":"Hey folks.<br /> <br /> I finally got the chance to run WtF (1E) last night and had some fun. I'm running a tweaked version of Welcome to Manitou Springs (the first part of the demo) and I don't have access to the full rules. Consequently, a couple of situations occurred during play that I had trouble dealing with.<br /> <br /> First off, the players tracked a glass Claimed to a convenience store of a gas station. In addition to the Claimed, the station's owner was also in the store at the time. The players tried to get the owner out of the store by lying- what were they supposed to roll? and is it contested? also, if the lie is corroborated by evidence (i.e. a character said they got attacked by wolves and they were covered in blood), how is it represented in game terms? a bonus to the roll?<br /> <br /> After one of the characters walked with the owner out of the store, the other one attacked the Claimed inside. On the first turn, the attacking player transformed into Guru. As we were figuring out his dice pool for the attack, we noticed a bite attack does more damage than a claw attack. From a purely mechanical point of a view, is there any<br /> reason for a character to use a claw instead of a bite while in attacking in Guru? <br /> <br /> After the player attacked, the Claimed sprouted glass shards out of his body. In addition to other effects, the shards damage anyone who hits the Claimed with unarmed attacks so the player tried to throw the Claimed against the counter. What would you have the player roll in that situation?<br /> <br /> After a couple of turns, the Claimed was down to his last health box and tried to run. What was Claimed supposed to roll? Was the player allowed to try and catch him somehow? And how does Speed figure into all of this, if at all?<br /> <br /> Thanks a bunch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12374-darkemor","name":"Darkemor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-03T10:12:37-07:00","id":962872,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkemor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/962750-1e-newbie-questions#post962750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey folks.<br /> <br /> I finally got the chance to run WtF (1E) last night and had some fun. I'm running a tweaked version of Welcome to Manitou Springs (the first part of the demo) and I don't have access to the full rules. Consequently, a couple of situations occurred during play that I had trouble dealing with.<br /> <br /> First off, the players tracked a glass Claimed to a convenience store of a gas station. In addition to the Claimed, the station's owner was also in the store at the time. The players tried to get the owner out of the store by lying- what were they supposed to roll? and is it contested? also, if the lie is corroborated by evidence (i.e. a character said they got attacked by wolves and they were covered in blood), how is it represented in game terms? a bonus to the roll?</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably Presence or Manipulation (depending on their approach) + Subterfuge. In general, Subterfuge is the lying Skill. It might be Contested by Wits + Empathy or Subterfuge, depending on how he's trying to assess if they're lying. If you've got evidence to support your lie, it might be an equipment bonus to the roll, or it might make it auto-succeed, depending on how convincing the evidence is.<br /> <br /> In the Storytelling system, you have to adjudicate a lot based on the particular circumstances and your best discretion. When the players attempt something, ask yourself if it's possible to succeed (if it's not, it fails), if it's possible to fail (if not, it succeeds), if there are consequences for failure (read: if there's no reason they can't just keep trying until they eventually succeed, then don't bother making them roll a bunch of times - if time is of the essence, lost time does count as a consequence of failure). If the answer to all of these questions is yes, ask for a roll that feels appropriate to the task (this is where a deep and intuitive understanding of what each of the Attributes and Skills are for is important). Add bonuses and/or penalties to the dice pool as necessary based on any circumstances outside of the players' own abilities (tools or obstacles, for example).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkemor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/962750-1e-newbie-questions#post962750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After one of the characters walked with the owner out of the store, the other one attacked the Claimed inside. On the first turn, the attacking player transformed into Guru. As we were figuring out his dice pool for the attack, we noticed a bite attack does more damage than a claw attack. From a purely mechanical point of a view, is there any<br /> reason for a character to use a claw instead of a bite while in attacking in Guru?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, for one thing, there are plenty of things you might want to attack, but don't want to put in your mouth. Sometimes this is a mechanical concern, such as if you don't want to risk swallowing a vampire's blood, or a human's flesh. Most of the time, however, a bite will be better. That's pretty appropriate if you think about it, wolves do a lot of biting and not a lot of clawing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkemor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/962750-1e-newbie-questions#post962750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After the player attacked, the Claimed sprouted glass shards out of his body. In addition to other effects, the shards damage anyone who hits the Claimed with unarmed attacks so the player tried to throw the Claimed against the counter. What would you have the player roll in that situation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Throwing someone is a Grapple move. I don't remember the 1e grapple rules off the top of my head, but look those up and it should say how to throw someone you're grappling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkemor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/962750-1e-newbie-questions#post962750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After a couple of turns, the Claimed was down to his last health box and tried to run. What was Claimed supposed to roll? Was the player allowed to try and catch him somehow? And how does Speed figure into all of this, if at all?</div> </div> </div> </div>Speed determines how far you can move in a combat turn, so it's directly relavent as long as you're still in combat turns. A footchase is one of the standard example actions in the Skills section of the core rulebook; if I recall correctly it's an extended and contested roll of Stamina + Athletics vs. Stamina + Athletics. Can't remember the default targe number, but you should be able to look it up. So,?at what ever point you decide the Claimed is far enough from the fray to switch from combat turns to a chase scene, that's what you'd use.<br /> <br /> 2e has much more detailed systems for both Social interaction and chase scenes, much cleaner grapple rules, and 2e Gauru form's teeth and claws have the same damage rating. I'd highly recommend 2e.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-03T10:19:16-07:00","id":962876,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darkemor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/962750-1e-newbie-questions#post962750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">HI finally got the chance to run WtF (1E) last night and had some fun. I'm running a tweaked version of Welcome to Manitou Springs (the first part of the demo) and I don't have access to the full rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>Awesome! Glad you had fun. If you do end up getting the core book, I strongly suggest <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/145541/Werewolf-the-Forsaken-2nd-Edition\" target=\"_blank\">second edition</a>. It's more of a stand alone book but it really fixes a lot of problems with first edition as well as giving more focus for the players.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First off, the players tracked a glass Claimed to a convenience store of a gas station. In addition to the Claimed, the station's owner was also in the store at the time. The players tried to get the owner out of the store by lying- what were they supposed to roll? and is it contested? also, if the lie is corroborated by evidence (i.e. a character said they got attacked by wolves and they were covered in blood), how is it represented in game terms? a bonus to the roll? </div> </div> </div>Depending on how they were lying, Presence or Manipulation + Persuasion. Yes, certain things would be bonuses or penalties, that would be a bonus.<br /> <br /> Just for future reference, most rolls in the game are going to be Attribute + Skill + or - Modifiers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> After one of the characters walked with the owner out of the store, the other one attacked the Claimed inside. On the first turn, the attacking player transformed into Guru. As we were figuring out his dice pool for the attack, we noticed a bite attack does more damage than a claw attack. From a purely mechanical point of a view, is there any reason for a character to use a claw instead of a bite while in attacking in Guru? </div> </div> </div>Just if you don't want to bite it. Some werewolves will get triggered into Kuruth if they taste human blood. Some enemies have toxic blood, or other fluids you don't want to get in your mouth. Some gauru even use weapons, and there's a Living Weapon Merit that allows you to improve the efficiency of your Claws or your Bite, so your Claw attack can become better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> After the player attacked, the Claimed sprouted glass shards out of his body. In addition to other effects, the shards damage anyone who hits the Claimed with unarmed attacks so the player tried to throw the Claimed against the counter. What would you have the player roll in that situation? </div> </div> </div>You could probably justify it as a physical attack, if it was more of a 'shove,' in which case it would be Strength + Brawl. Otherwise perhaps as a Grapple, which is also Strength + Brawl.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> After a couple of turns, the Claimed was down to his last health box and tried to run. What was Claimed supposed to roll? Was the player allowed to try and catch him somehow? And how does Speed figure into all of this, if at all? </div> </div> </div>Traditionally it would be Stamina + Athletics versus Stamina + Athletics, extended and contested action, with each roll being a turn of running. The Claimed's Successes must match or overcome the pursuer's Speed to get away, while the werewolf needs to get the Claimed's successes. For every three points of difference between the competitors' Speed traits, the faster one gets a +1 bonus on their rolls.<br /> <br /> The <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/168428/Chronicles-of-Darkness\" target=\"_blank\">Chronicles of Darkness book</a> has some more fleshed out Chase rules that people seem to like but I don't know them off hand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-03T14:41:47-07:00","id":962972,"title":"","content":"Wow, thank you Charlaquin and nofather for the informative and helpful replies.<br /> I'll be sure to give 2nd edition a look after I finish running the first part of the demo-<br /> I tweaked the demo so I could run it as a one shot <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> --Darkemor","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12374-darkemor","name":"Darkemor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}